requirements:
Implement in Rust as a SINGLE source file that compiles with rustc --edition 2024, std only. Do not use inner attributes (#![...]) or inner documentation (//!...). Output ONLY the Rust source code inside ```rust fences.
All listed types and methods must be public. All fields of the listed structs must be public. Listed structs and error enums must implement Clone, Debug, PartialEq, and Eq. Internal state types need not implement those traits. No operation may panic for any input representable by the API.
Implement a deterministic priority job scheduler with explicit millisecond times.
Public API:
#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)] pub struct Job { pub id: u64, pub priority: u32, pub ready_at: u64 }
#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)] pub struct Dispatch { pub job: Job, pub enqueued_at: u64, pub started_at: u64, pub effective_priority: u32 }
#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)] pub enum SchedulerError { ZeroAgingInterval, TimeWentBackwards, DuplicateId(u64) }
pub struct Scheduler (implementation-defined private fields)
impl Scheduler {
  pub fn new(aging_interval_ms: u64, start_ms: u64) -> Result<Self, SchedulerError>;
  pub fn enqueue(&mut self, job: Job, now: u64) -> Result<(), SchedulerError>;
  pub fn next_ready(&mut self, now: u64) -> Result<Option<Dispatch>, SchedulerError>;
  pub fn cancel(&mut self, id: u64, now: u64) -> Result<Option<Job>, SchedulerError>;
  pub fn len(&self) -> usize;
}

new rejects aging_interval_ms==0 with ZeroAgingInterval. Start empty with one global clock start_ms. All time-taking methods first check that now >= the last successful operation's time, else TimeWentBackwards. Equal timestamps are valid. ALL errors leave ALL scheduler state unchanged, including the clock. Successful operations advance the clock to now, including next_ready returning None and cancel returning None. len counts all pending jobs (ready and future) and does not advance time.
enqueue then checks whether id is currently pending; if so return DuplicateId(id). The backwards-time error takes precedence over DuplicateId. Any u64 ID, any u32 priority, and any ready_at value are valid, including times before enqueue and u64::MAX. Record enqueued_at=now and a FIFO insertion position. Jobs enqueued in separate calls at the same timestamp retain their call order. A dispatched or cancelled ID may be enqueued again as a NEW insertion with a NEW enqueue timestamp.
next_ready selects only pending jobs with ready_at <= now. For each eligible job define age_steps=floor((now-enqueued_at)/aging_interval_ms) and effective_priority=min(u32::MAX, job.priority+age_steps), using exact non-wrapping arithmetic. Aging begins at enqueue, including time spent waiting for ready_at; it does NOT start at ready_at and cannot accrue before enqueue. Larger effective priorities win. Among equal effective priorities, choose the job enqueued earliest in insertion order, regardless of base priority, ID, ready_at, or age. Saturated priorities also tie by FIFO. Remove exactly the selected job and return Dispatch with its unchanged original Job, original enqueued_at, started_at=now, and computed effective_priority. Return None when no job is ready, retaining all jobs; never advance to a future ready_at automatically. Recompute priorities for the supplied now on every call.
cancel removes and returns the original Job for the given pending ID, or None if absent, regardless of readiness. Cancellation preserves relative FIFO order of remaining jobs. All times through u64::MAX and waits larger than u32::MAX aging steps must work without overflow. No clocks, threads, sleeps, external crates, or nondeterministic ordering.
